{
  "dom_id": "step-q101458",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14133433/assets/2fd396c9a62f29969acd4af3de44ac59dd4851a31b77e0c7902ab2e023942e31.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of heptagons to squares is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ heptagon for every $\\boxed{}$ squares.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we count the number of heptagons. We see that there are $4$ heptagons.\n\nThen, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $4$ squares.\n\nSo the ratio of heptagons to squares is $4:4.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,4 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $4:4.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of heptagons to squares is $1:1$ because there is $1$ heptagon for every $1$ square.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
